
# Mixed into every invite code. The hosted runtime exposes no `crypto` and
# hands out sequential integer row ids, so this secret is the only thing that
# makes a code unguessable — see `inviteCode` in `server/index.ts`. Any long
# random string will do; changing it does not invalidate existing codes, which
# are stored, not recomputed.
INVITE_SECRET=

# Who may open the admin console (D62) — a comma- or whitespace-separated list
# of account user ids, as `ctx.auth.userId` reports them (`account:…`).
#
# Fail-closed: absent or empty means **nobody** is an administrator, and there
# is no bootstrap path — the console can delete a household, so nothing in the
# UI grants this and nothing ever should.
#
# **There is no dev bypass any more.** It used to admit `sf dev`'s guest
# automatically, and that leaked the console to the whole internet in v15,
# because the hosted runtime hands an unauthenticated caller the same identity.
# To administer locally, list your `?guest=` id here — `guest:you-<random>` —
# beside your real `account:` one. Both ship; only one can exist on each side.
LARDER_ADMIN_IDS=

# Which `?guest=<name>` identities `sf dev` may sign in as. Names, not ids.
#
# `sf dev` has no sign-in, so it mints an identity from a `?guest=` parameter:
# `http://127.0.0.1:4173/?guest=you-3f9a2c81`. Two names are two people, which
# is how invites, roles and the console's People list get tested without a
# second real account.
#
# `local` is refused however it is written — that is the identity a *published*
# space hands an unauthenticated caller, and admitting it is what leaked v15.
# Give each name a random suffix and treat it as a secret: a published space
# cannot mint a named guest today, and the suffix is what keeps this safe if
# that ever changes.
LARDER_DEV_GUESTS=

# How long admin console audit rows are kept, in months (D62). Default 24;
# 0 keeps nothing, and anything unparseable or out of range falls back.
#
# **Deliberately not a control in the console.** An administrator who can
# shorten retention can erase the record of what administrators did — the same
# failure as a console that could mint administrators, and it gets the same
# answer: the knob is set out of band and the console only reports it.
#
# Enforcement is append-time: the log prunes what has expired every time it is
# written to, so a log nothing is adding to is a log nothing is pruning.
LARDER_RETENTION_MONTHS=24
